{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3188","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3188","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3188\",\"parent_revision\":\"eabf7ed840da1afbf87f818e539900a93f75ad22\",\"root_revision\":\"d5982b5dd6da5a3b7b3fb64054257f73ba74563dceb418c3f4b7a8c187883e35\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`backlog_core/operations.py:2711` guards the branch that exists to preserve real entry IDs when reading an item:\\n\\n```python\\nbody_header_names = {span.name for span in body_spans}\\nyaml_section_names = set(item.sections.keys())\\nif body_header_names.issubset(yaml_section_names):\\n    # YAML covers all body headers \u2014 use it to preserve real entry IDs.\\n    return _build_sections_from_yaml_item(item)\\n```\\n\\n`body_header_names` holds display titles taken from the markdown body \u2014 `Acceptance Criteria`, `Root Cause Analysis`. `yaml_section_names` holds the stored section keys, which are snake_case. Verified against real stored data in `backlog_core/tests/fixtures/issue-3152.yaml`, whose section keys are `root_cause_analysis` and `acceptance_criteria`.\\n\\n`{\\\"Acceptance Criteria\\\"}.issubset({\\\"acceptance_criteria\\\", ...})` is always False. For any item with a multi-word section \u2014 which is nearly all of them \u2014 the branch is unreachable and control falls through to `_build_sections_metadata(body, ...)`, which reparses the body.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe comment states the branch's purpose: preserve real entry IDs. Because it never executes, IDs are re-derived from the body on every read instead of being read from storage. The corruption the branch exists to prevent therefore happens anyway, on every read of every item with a multi-word section.\\n\\nThis compounds with the entry-ID design. A re-derived positional ID is unstable: deleting a sibling entry changes which entry a given `-N` suffix targets, so an ID a caller obtained from one read may address a different entry on the next. Single-word section names satisfy the subset check by coincidence and take the intended path, which means the two behaviours coexist in one tracker with no visible difference to a caller.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation during messy-read risk analysis, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Not Established\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nWhether the comparison was ever correct \u2014 that is, whether stored keys were once display-cased and later normalized to snake_case, or whether the branch has never executed since it was written. `git log -L` over the branch and over the key-normalization code would settle it.\\n\\nWhether any consumer currently depends on the fall-through behaviour. Fixing the comparison changes which code path every multi-word-section read takes, so the branch's output has to be shown equivalent before the guard is corrected.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nWhether the comparison was ever correct \u2014 that is, whether stored keys were once display-cased and later normalized to snake_case, or whether the branch has never executed since it was written. `git log -L` over the branch and over the key-normalization code would settle it.\\n\\nWhether any consumer currently depends on the fall-through behaviour. Fixing the comparison changes which code path every multi-word-section read takes, so the branch's output has to be shown equivalent before the guard is corrected.\\n</div>\\n\\n## User Provided Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nNormalizing both sides of the comparison through the existing section-key resolution helper is the visible direction. The wider question grooming should settle is why a display name and a storage key are being compared directly at all, given the repo already has a resolver for exactly this \u2014 the same mismatch appears independently in `strike_entry`, filed separately.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nNormalizing both sides of the comparison through the existing section-key resolution helper is the visible direction. The wider question grooming should settle is why a display name and a storage key are being compared directly at all, given the repo already has a resolver for exactly this \u2014 the same mismatch appears independently in `strike_entry`, filed separately.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"a3ed74b2b2d2812f9c6c088ff120c3d40c7fc99159d9ff1a8755c511c5c34ab4\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQTSCqQ\"}"}